---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CalculatorIsland from '../components/CalculatorIsland.astro';
import MedicalDisclaimer from '../components/MedicalDisclaimer.astro';
---

<Layout
	title="GFR Calculator — Free Online eGFR & Kidney Function Calculator"
	description="Free GFR calculator with weight, creatinine, and age. Get instant eGFR results using CKD-EPI, MDRD, or Cockcroft-Gault formulas, with full step-by-step math and unit conversion."
>
	<section class="container-page pt-14 pb-8 sm:pt-20">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl text-center">
			<p class="font-mono text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-mute">Online eGFR &amp; Creatinine Clearance Calculator</p>
			<h1 class="mt-3 text-[2.25rem] font-semibold leading-[1.1] tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-5xl sm:tracking-tighter">
				GFR Calculator: Estimate Your Kidney Function
			</h1>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-body sm:text-lg">
				A free GFR calculator with weight, creatinine, and age inputs — computing eGFR with
				CKD-EPI 2021, CKD-EPI 2009, MDRD, or Cockcroft-Gault. Full unit conversion and
				step-by-step math shown for every result.
			</p>
			<nav aria-label="Site information" class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-4 gap-y-1.5 text-sm">
				<a href="/methodology" class="text-body hover:text-ink hover:underline">Methodology</a>
				<span class="text-hairline" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
				<a href="/about" class="text-body hover:text-ink hover:underline">About Us</a>
				<span class="text-hairline" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
				<a href="/privacy-policy" class="text-body hover:text-ink hover:underline">Privacy Policy</a>
				<span class="text-hairline" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
				<a href="/terms" class="text-body hover:text-ink hover:underline">Terms &amp; Conditions</a>
				<span class="text-hairline" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
				<a href="/contact" class="text-body hover:text-ink hover:underline">Contact Us</a>
			</nav>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="container-page pb-16">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
			<CalculatorIsland />
			<div class="mt-6">
				<MedicalDisclaimer />
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="border-t border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated py-16">
		<div class="container-page">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl text-center">
				<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
					Built to be verifiably accurate
				</h2>
				<p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
					A peer-reviewed 2020 study tested 47 freely available online GFR calculators and found
					that 8 of them (17%) produced erroneous results — mostly from mishandling unit
					conversions and the old race-based CKD-EPI formula.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="mx-auto mt-10 grid max-w-3xl gap-6 sm:grid-cols-3">
				<div class="rounded-md border border-hairline p-5">
					<div class="flex h-9 w-9 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-link-soft text-link-deep">
						<svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
							<path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4M12 3l7 4v5c0 5-3.5 8-7 9-3.5-1-7-4-7-9V7l7-4Z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round"/>
						</svg>
					</div>
					<h3 class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Tested unit conversion</h3>
					<p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
						mg/dL &harr; &micro;mol/L conversion is centralized in one function and covered by
						automated tests in both directions.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="rounded-md border border-hairline p-5">
					<div class="flex h-9 w-9 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-link-soft text-link-deep">
						<svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
							<path d="M4 6h16M4 12h10M4 18h16" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round"/>
						</svg>
					</div>
					<h3 class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Full step-by-step math</h3>
					<p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
						Every result shows the formula with your actual numbers substituted in, so you can
						verify the calculation yourself.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="rounded-md border border-hairline p-5">
					<div class="flex h-9 w-9 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-link-soft text-link-deep">
						<svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
							<path d="M12 21s-7-4.5-9-9.5C1.5 7 4 3.5 8 4.5c1.7.4 3 1.7 4 3 1-1.3 2.3-2.6 4-3 4-1 6.5 2.5 5 7-2 5-9 9.5-9 9.5Z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
						</svg>
					</div>
					<h3 class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Race-free by default</h3>
					<p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
						Follows the 2021 NKF/ASN recommendation to remove race from eGFR estimation across
						every formula on this site.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="mt-8 text-center">
				<a href="/methodology" class="text-sm font-medium text-link hover:text-link-deep">
					Read our full methodology &rarr;
				</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="border-t border-hairline py-16">
		<div class="container-page">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
				<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
					What is a GFR calculator?
				</h2>
				<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
					A GFR calculator estimates your glomerular filtration rate (GFR) — the rate at which
					your kidneys filter waste from your blood — using a simple blood test result called
					serum creatinine, along with your age and sex. Because true GFR requires an invasive
					clearance test, doctors normally rely on an <strong>eGFR calculator</strong> to estimate
					it from routine labs. This tool is a free <strong>GFR calculator online</strong> that
					works entirely in your browser: no sign-up, no data sent to a server, and no ads
					between you and your result.
				</p>

				<h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold text-ink">GFR calculator formula options</h3>
				<p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
					There is no single universal <strong>GFR calculator formula</strong> — different
					equations trade off accuracy, required inputs, and clinical use case. This
					<strong>CKD-EPI calculator</strong> implements both the 2021 race-free equation (the
					current NKF/ASN-recommended standard) and the older 2009 version some lab reports still
					cite. The <strong>MDRD calculator</strong> mode reproduces the widely used four-variable
					MDRD study equation, which tends to underestimate GFR above roughly 60 mL/min/1.73m&sup2;
					but remains common on older lab panels. For drug dosing and situations where a
					weight-based estimate of creatinine clearance is preferred over a body-surface-area
					normalized eGFR, switch to <strong>Cockcroft-Gault calculator</strong> mode — this is
					the only <strong>GFR calculator with weight</strong> as a required input, since the
					Cockcroft-Gault equation was originally derived to estimate creatinine clearance in
					mL/min rather than a normalized eGFR.
				</p>

				<h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold text-ink">Using the GFR calculator with creatinine, weight, and age</h3>
				<p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
					Every formula on this page is a <strong>GFR calculator with creatinine</strong> at its
					core — you enter your serum creatinine value (in mg/dL or &micro;mol/L, with automatic
					conversion), your age, and your sex. Switch to Cockcroft-Gault when you also want a
					<strong>GFR calculator with weight</strong> factored in. Whichever formula you choose,
					the result panel shows the equation with your actual numbers substituted in step by
					step, so you or your clinician can verify the arithmetic by hand rather than trusting a
					black-box number.
				</p>

				<h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold text-ink">A note on pediatric GFR</h3>
				<p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
					CKD-EPI, MDRD, and Cockcroft-Gault were all derived and validated on adult populations,
					so this calculator requires an age of 18 or older. A <strong>GFR calculator pediatric</strong>
					use case needs a different equation entirely — the bedside Schwartz formula, which uses
					height instead of the adult-specific coefficients above — and is intentionally out of
					scope here so that this tool never returns a number that looks precise but is not
					clinically valid for children.
				</p>

				<h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold text-ink">Interpreting your kidney function calculator result</h3>
				<p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
					eGFR results from CKD-EPI and MDRD are reported in mL/min/1.73m&sup2; and map to the
					KDIGO CKD stages, from G1 (normal or high function, &ge; 90) down to G5 (kidney
					failure, &lt; 15). Cockcroft-Gault results are reported in mL/min and are not directly
					comparable to those staged values, since it is not normalized to body surface area. A
					single low reading on any <strong>kidney function calculator</strong> is not, by itself,
					a CKD diagnosis — KDIGO guidance requires reduced eGFR or another marker of kidney
					damage, such as albuminuria, to persist for three months or more before CKD is
					diagnosed. If your result is unexpectedly low, or you have risk factors like diabetes,
					hypertension, or a family history of kidney disease, discuss it with a clinician who can
					order a repeat test and any additional markers needed rather than relying on a single
					calculation.
				</p>

				<h3 class="mt-8 text-lg font-semibold text-ink">Why this GFR calculator formula matters for accuracy</h3>
				<p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
					A 2020 peer-reviewed study found that 8 of 47 freely available online
					<strong>eGFR calculator</strong> and <strong>creatinine clearance calculator</strong>
					tools produced outright erroneous results, mostly from silent unit-conversion bugs and
					the deprecated race-based CKD-EPI coefficient. Rather than claiming to be the
					<strong>best GFR calculator</strong> on marketing alone, this
					<strong>kidney function calculator</strong> centralizes unit conversion into one tested
					function, omits the race coefficient consistent with the 2021 NKF/ASN recommendation,
					shows its work on every calculation, and cites its sources on the
					<a href="/methodology" class="text-link hover:text-link-deep">methodology page</a> so
					you can judge its accuracy for yourself instead of taking it on faith.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="border-t border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated py-16">
		<div class="container-page">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
				<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
					Frequently asked questions
				</h2>

				<div class="mt-8 divide-y divide-hairline border-y border-hairline">
					<details class="group py-5">
						<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-base font-medium text-ink">
							What is the most accurate GFR calculator?
							<svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-mute transition-transform group-open:rotate-45" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
								<path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/>
							</svg>
						</summary>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
							No online calculator is more accurate than the formula it implements, since every
							GFR calculator is only estimating kidney function from the same handful of
							equations. CKD-EPI 2021 is currently considered the most accurate general-purpose
							formula and is the one recommended by the NKF and ASN, because it was validated on
							a larger, more diverse population than MDRD and removed the race coefficient that
							caused systematic errors in Black patients. Accuracy also depends on the
							calculator's implementation — a 2020 study found 8 of 47 freely available online
							GFR calculators produced erroneous results due to unit-conversion bugs, not formula
							choice.
						</p>
					</details>

					<details class="group py-5">
						<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-base font-medium text-ink">
							What is the best GFR calculator?
							<svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-mute transition-transform group-open:rotate-45" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
								<path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/>
							</svg>
						</summary>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
							The best GFR calculator for you depends on why you need the number. For general
							kidney-function screening, a CKD-EPI 2021 calculator is the current standard. For
							drug-dosing decisions, a Cockcroft-Gault calculator with weight is usually
							preferred because that is the population the dosing guidelines were built on.
							Beyond formula choice, look for a calculator that shows its unit conversion and
							step-by-step math so you can verify the result yourself, rather than trusting an
							unexplained number.
						</p>
					</details>

					<details class="group py-5">
						<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-base font-medium text-ink">
							How to calculate GFR formula?
							<svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-mute transition-transform group-open:rotate-45" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
								<path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/>
							</svg>
						</summary>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
							Every GFR formula on this site takes the same core inputs — serum creatinine, age,
							and sex — and plugs them into a fixed equation. For CKD-EPI 2021, you first divide
							your creatinine by a sex-specific constant (&kappa;), raise the min and max of that
							ratio to fixed exponents, multiply by 142, then apply an age-decay term
							(0.9938 raised to your age) and a small multiplier if female. Cockcroft-Gault
							instead multiplies (140 minus age) by weight in kg, divides by 72 times serum
							creatinine, and applies a 0.85 multiplier if female. Rather than computing this by
							hand, enter your values above and the calculator shows every intermediate step of
							whichever formula you choose.
						</p>
					</details>

					<details class="group py-5">
						<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-base font-medium text-ink">
							What is the GFR calculator formula?
							<svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-mute transition-transform group-open:rotate-45" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
								<path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/>
							</svg>
						</summary>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
							There isn't a single GFR calculator formula — this site implements four: CKD-EPI
							2021 (race-free, current NKF/ASN standard), CKD-EPI 2009 (the predecessor equation,
							still referenced on some lab reports), MDRD (an older four-variable equation), and
							Cockcroft-Gault (a weight-based creatinine clearance estimate used mainly for drug
							dosing). Full formula definitions, constants, and literature sources for each are
							documented on the <a href="/methodology" class="text-link hover:text-link-deep">methodology page</a>.
						</p>
					</details>

					<details class="group py-5">
						<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-base font-medium text-ink">
							How does a GFR calculator with weight work?
							<svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-mute transition-transform group-open:rotate-45" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
								<path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/>
							</svg>
						</summary>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
							A GFR calculator with weight uses the Cockcroft-Gault equation, which estimates
							creatinine clearance in mL/min directly from body weight rather than normalizing to
							a standard 1.73m&sup2; body surface area like CKD-EPI and MDRD do. It multiplies
							(140 minus age) by weight in kilograms, divides by 72 times serum creatinine in
							mg/dL, and applies a 0.85 multiplier for female patients. Because it uses actual
							body weight, it's the formula most drug-dosing guidelines were originally built
							around.
						</p>
					</details>

					<details class="group py-5">
						<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-base font-medium text-ink">
							What is a GFR calculator with creatinine?
							<svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-mute transition-transform group-open:rotate-45" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
								<path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/>
							</svg>
						</summary>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
							Every formula on this page is a GFR calculator with creatinine, since serum
							creatinine is the core lab value all four equations are built on. You can enter
							creatinine in either mg/dL (common in the US) or &micro;mol/L (common elsewhere),
							and the calculator converts between them automatically using the standard factor
							of 1 mg/dL &asymp; 88.4 &micro;mol/L before running any formula.
						</p>
					</details>

					<details class="group py-5">
						<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-base font-medium text-ink">
							Is there a GFR calculator for pediatric patients?
							<svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-mute transition-transform group-open:rotate-45" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
								<path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/>
							</svg>
						</summary>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
							Not on this site. CKD-EPI, MDRD, and Cockcroft-Gault were all derived and validated
							on adults, so this calculator requires an age of 18 or older. Pediatric GFR
							estimation needs a different equation — the bedside Schwartz formula, which uses
							height rather than the adult-specific constants above — and is intentionally out
							of scope here so the tool never returns a number that looks precise but isn't
							clinically valid for a child.
						</p>
					</details>

					<details class="group py-5">
						<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-base font-medium text-ink">
							What is the Cockcroft-Gault GFR calculator?
							<svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-mute transition-transform group-open:rotate-45" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
								<path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/>
							</svg>
						</summary>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
							The Cockcroft-Gault calculator estimates creatinine clearance in mL/min using age,
							sex, weight, and serum creatinine: CrCl = [(140 &minus; Age) &times; Weight(kg)] /
							(72 &times; Scr), multiplied by 0.85 for female patients. Published in 1976, it
							predates CKD-EPI and MDRD and reports an un-normalized clearance rather than a
							body-surface-area-indexed eGFR, which is why it's still the reference formula for
							many drug-dosing guidelines.
						</p>
					</details>

					<details class="group py-5">
						<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-base font-medium text-ink">
							What is the CKD-EPI GFR calculator?
							<svg class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-mute transition-transform group-open:rotate-45" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
								<path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/>
							</svg>
						</summary>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
							The CKD-EPI calculator estimates GFR in mL/min/1.73m&sup2; using serum creatinine,
							age, and sex. This site implements both the 2021 race-free version — the current
							NKF/ASN-recommended standard — and the original 2009 version some older lab
							reports still cite. CKD-EPI 2021 removed the race coefficient present in the 2009
							equation after the NKF/ASN Task Force found it systematically overestimated kidney
							function in Black patients.
						</p>
					</details>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Layout>

